SH(1)                       User Commands                      SH(1)

NAME
    sh - MiniOS command interpreter

SYNOPSIS
    sh [-c command [name [argument...]]]
    sh [script [argument...]]

DESCRIPTION
    sh reads commands interactively, from a script, or from -c. It supports
    pipelines, foreground and background process groups, redirections, quoted
    words, parameter expansion, command substitution, and conditional lists.

JOB CONTROL
    Append & to run a pipeline in the background. Ctrl-Z stops the foreground
    pipeline. jobs lists jobs; bg [%job] resumes a job in the background; fg
    [%job] resumes it in the foreground. With no operand, fg and bg select the
    most recently allocated job. A numeric job may be written as 2 or %2.

BUILTINS
    cd, exit, pwd, export, unset, set, jobs, fg, bg, wait, true, false,
    ulimit, help, test, [, echo, read, shift, local, return, source, .,
    alias, unalias, eval, type, command, break, continue, history, :,
    umask.

    umask prints the file creation mask in octal, or with -S as the
    permissions it leaves, and umask MODE sets it from an octal mode.

EXPANSIONS
    Expansion happens when a command executes: tilde, parameters, arithmetic
    $((expression)), command substitution $(command), splitting on IFS,
    filename patterns (* ? [...]), then quote removal. Single quotes protect
    everything; double quotes allow expansion without splitting or globbing.
    "$@" preserves separate arguments. ${name:-word}, :=, :+, :?, #, ##, %,
    and %% provide defaults, assignment, checks and pattern trimming.

CONTROL FLOW
    if commands; then commands; elif commands; then commands; else commands; fi
    for name in words; do commands; done
    while commands; do commands; done
    until commands; do commands; done
    case word in pattern|pattern) commands;; pattern) commands;; esac
    name() { commands; }
    (commands) runs in a subshell; { commands; } runs in the current shell.
    Functions have their own positional arguments. local creates a binding
    restored when the function returns. break and continue accept a loop count.

REDIRECTIONS
    [fd]<file, [fd]>file, [fd]>>file, [fd]<&number and [fd]>&number are applied
    from left to right. A descriptor target of - closes it. <<delimiter reads
    a here document; quote the delimiter to suppress expansion. <<- strips
    leading tabs. Builtin and compound-command redirections are restored
    after execution.

SEE ALSO
    find(1), xargs(1), pager(1), man(1)

INTERACTIVE EDITING
    Arrows move or recall history. Home/End and Ctrl-A/E move to line ends;
    Ctrl-B/F move a character, Alt-B/F a word. Backspace/Ctrl-H and Delete
    erase characters; Ctrl-K erases to the end, Ctrl-U to the start, Ctrl-W
    the preceding word. Tab completes paths and executable names. Ctrl-R
    searches backwards through history; repeat it for earlier matches.
    Ctrl-C cancels a line, Ctrl-D on an empty line exits, Ctrl-L redraws.

STARTUP AND ENVIRONMENT
    Interactive shells read /etc/profile and then $HOME/.shrc. HOME defaults
    to /home in init and Terminal. History is stored in HISTFILE, default
    $HOME/.sh_history, with HISTSIZE entries (default 500).
    PS1 supports \u user, \h host, \w directory, \W basename, \$ prompt,
    \t time, \n newline, \e escape, \[ and \] invisible-sequence markers,
    and \\ for a backslash. The built-in default is '\w \$ '. PS2 defaults
    to '> ' for incomplete commands. The profile adds colour when TERM
    permits it. $! is the most recently started background pipeline's PID.
